\section{What makes this different from the BMF}{

The BMF holds one current name per EIN. This holds one per filing year, so it
is the first source here that can show a name \emph{changing}. That is what makes
\code{variant_class = 'former'} derivable rather than aspirational: within an EIN,
the spelling carried by the most recent filing is \code{legal} and every earlier
distinct spelling is \code{former}, which is exactly the definition in section 3a.
}

\section{What is deliberately not ingested}{

\verb{F9_00_PRIN_OFF_*} looks like a principal \emph{office} address and is not - it is
the principal \strong{officer}, as the neighbouring \code{F9_00_PRIN_OFF_NAME_PERS}
makes plain. Those rows are a natural person's name and address, and where the
officer is an organisation it is a \emph{different} organisation from the filer, so
attaching its address to the filer's EIN would be wrong on top of intrusive.

A consequence worth stating: \code{address.variant_class = 'principal'} stays empty
after this ingest. The 990 header does not publish a principal-office address
distinct from the mailing address, and an empty class is a more honest answer
than one filled with somebody's home address.
